tout court, tout ensemble. See

| pronunciation. See also FALSE FRENCH WORDS.

tow- & towing-.
===
**tout court, tout ensemble.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.

tow- & towing-.
>>>

<<<
tow- & towing-. There is perhaps an impression that in the compounds

| Trade mark & trade union, no hy- (e.g. -boat, -line, -net, -path, -post,

| phens, see Hypuens 3B. In -rope) towing- is the correct form, & tow- a slovenly modern abbreviation.
===
**tow- & towing-.** There is perhaps an impression that in the compounds (e.g. *-boat, -line, -net, -path, -post, -rope*) *towing-* is the correct form, & *tow-* a slovenly modern abbreviation.
>>>

<<<
But it appears from the OED that tow-boat & tow-line are the only forms recorded for boat & line (the latter 1719), & tow-rope is about a century older than towing-rope; towing-path, however, is as much older than tow-path.
===
But it appears from the OED that *tow-boat* & *tow-line* are the only forms recorded for boat & line (the latter 1719), & *tow-rope* is about a century older than *towing-rope*; *towing-path*, however, is as much older than *tow-path*.
>>>

<<<
There is in fact no reason for avoiding either form. Cf. wash(ing)-stand.
===
There is in fact no reason for avoiding either form. Cf. *wash(ing)-stand*.
>>>

<<<
toward, towards, tcwardly. The adjectives toward (including the predicative use as in a storm is toward, i.e. coming) & towardly are pronounced td’ard(li).
===
**toward, towards, towardly.** The adjectives *toward* (including the predicative use as in *a storm is toward,* i.e. coming) & *towardly* are pronounced tō'ard(lĭ).
>>>

<<<
The prepositions are best pronounced tord(z), but in recent use the influence of spelling is forcing toowor’d(z) on the half educated.
===
The prepositions are best pronounced to͞rd(z), but in recent use the influence of spelling is forcing to͝owor'd(z) on the half educated.
>>>

<<<
towel makes -lled, -lling; -LL-, -L-.
===
**towel** makes *-lled, -lling*; ^^-LL-, -L-^^.
>>>

<<<
town. T. clerk, t. council, t. hall, t. house, & t. talk, should all be written as two separate words without hyphens; see Hypsrens 3 B.
===
**town.** *T. clerk, t. council, t. hall, t. house, & t. talk,* should all be written as two separate words without hyphens; see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B.
>>>

<<<
For town-councillor, which should be hyphened, see TRADE-unionist.
===
For *town-councillor*, which should be hyphened, see ^^TRADE^^-*unionist*.
>>>

<<<
toy, n. A toyshop or toy-shop is a shop where toys are sold; a child’s mock shop, on the other hand, is a toy shop; see HyPHENSs 8 B.
===
**toy, n.** A *toyshop* or *toy-shop* is a shop where toys are sold; a child's mock shop, on the other hand, is *toy shop*; see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B.
>>>

<<<
toy, v. Forinflexions see VERBS IN -IE &e., 4.
===
**toy, v.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE &c.^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
trace, n. For synonyms see SIGN.
===
**trace, n.** For synonyms see ^^SIGN^^.
>>>

<<<
trace, v., makes -ceable; see MUTE E.
===
**trace, v.,** makes *-ceable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
trachea. Pronounce traké’a; pl. tracheae. The derived word in -itis is tracheitis (4 syl.), not trachitis.
===
**trachea.** Pronounce trakē'a; pl. *tracheae*. The derived word in *-itis* is *tracheitis* (4 syl.), not *trachitis*.
>>>

<<<
But the reaction of the many de- Tivatives, such as tracheotomy & tracheoscopic, in which the relative stress on the first two syllables is reversed, has made tra’kia a popular
===
But the reaction of the many derivatives, such as *tracheotomy* & *tracheoscopic*, in which the relative stress on the first two syllables is reversed, has made trā'kĭ*a* a popular
>>>

<<<
has made trā'kĭ*a* a popular

+ QUANTITY.

trade. Writing of compounds. | trades(-)union
===
has made trā'kĭ*a* a popular

+ pronunciation. See also ^^FALSE QUANTITY^^.

**trade.** Writing of compounds. *Trade mark & trade union*, no hyphens, see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B. In *trades(-)union*
>>>

<<<
 (cf. swordsman, townsfolk, &c.) the question is not so simple, but, as that form is dying out, need not be answered.
===
 (cf. *swordsman, townsfolk,* &c.) the question is not so simple, but, as that form is dying out, need not be answered.
>>>

<<<
In trade-unionist the hyphen, which would be wrong in trade union, is right, since -ist belongs not to union alone, but to the compound; cf. such words as bow-legged, never bow | legged.
===
In *trade-unionist* the hyphen, which would be wrong in *trade union*, is right, since -ist belongs not to *union* alone, but to the compound; cf. such words as *bow-legged*, never *bow legged*.
>>>

<<<
though not obligatory (& not used in several of the OED quotations), is right if the stress is put, as the | OED marks it, on trade.
===
In *trade-wind* the hyphen, though not obligatory (& not used in several of the OED quotations), is right if the stress is put, as the OED marks it, on *trade*.
>>>

<<<
trade, v., makes -dable; see MUTE E.
===
**trade, v.,** makes *-dable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
trade-wind. See WIND, n.
===
**trade-wind.** See ^^WIND^^, n.
>>>

<<<
tradition(aljism, -ist. For general question between such variants, see -IsT.
===
**tradition(al)ism, -ist.** For the general question between such variants, see ^^-IST^^.
>>>

<<<
In this case the longer forms are usual, probably because the words are often opposed to rationalism, -ist, the form of which is fixed by ration’s not having the necessary meaning.
===
In this case the longer forms are usual, probably because the words are often opposed to *rationalism, -ist,* the form of which is fixed by *ration*'s not having the necessary meaning.
>>>

<<<
traduce makes -cible; see -ABLE 2.
===
**traduce** makes *-cible*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
traffic, v., makes -icked, -icking; see -C-, -CK-.
===
**traffic, v.,** makes *-icked, -icker, -icking*; see ^^-C-, -CK-^^.
>>>

<<<
tragédienne. See COMEDIAN.
===
**tragédienne.** See ^^COMEDIAN^^.
>>>

<<<
tragic(al). See -1c(au). It may almost be said that the longer form is, in serious use, dead; though the OED quotes it once or twice from modern writers in senses that it does | not mark obsolete, in each of them tragic would have been the natural word.
===
**tragic(al).** See ^^-IC(AL)^^. It may almost be said that the longer form is, in serious use, dead; though the OED quotes it once or twice from modern writers in senses that it does not mark obsolete, in each of them *tragic* would have been the natural word.
>>>

<<<
It survives, however, in playful use, often with a memory of the ‘ very tragicall mirth’ of Pyramus & Thisbe in Midsummer Nights Dream.
===
It survives, however, in playful use, often with a memory of the 'very tragicall mirth' of Pyramus & Thisbe in *Midsummer Night's Dream.*
>>>

<<<
For tragic (or dramatic) trony, see IRONY, 2.
===
For *tragic (or dramatic) irony,* see ^^IRONY^^, 2.
>>>

<<<
tragicomedy, tragicomic, &c. forms are due to medieval Latin, & too old to be themselves corrected to | tragico-comic &c.
===
**tragicomedy, tragicomic, &c.** The forms are due to medieval Latin, & too old to be themselves corrected to *tragico-comic* &c.
>>>

<<<
But such syncopations should be at least noted as irregular when opportunity offers, with a view to discouraging imita- _ tions. See pacifist in the article -1stT. In trade-wind the hyphen, ` the . -icker, — The |
===
But such syncopations should be at least noted as irregular when opportunity offers, with a view to discouraging imitations. See *pacifist* in the article ^^-IST^^.
>>>
